#!/bin/sh
# Copyright Amazon.com, Inc. or its affiliates. All Rights Reserved.
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT-0
#
# Refuse a push whose destination is develop or main.
#
# Install with `make install-git-hooks`, which copies this into the repository's
# hooks directory. It is tracked here rather than only in .git/hooks because git
# does not clone hooks, so an untracked one protects whoever happened to install
# it and nobody else.
#
# Why this exists alongside the PreToolUse hook (scripts/hooks/check_shared_branch.py):
# that one inspects commands the coding assistant runs, and a push has three other
# routes — a shell the assistant never sees, an IDE's push button, and the
# `git push` inside `make commit`. git supplies the resolved destination refs on
# stdin here, so no command-line parsing can get it wrong.
#
# TWO WAYS THIS HOOK IS REACHED, and why the second needs a fallback.
#
# Normally git runs it with the refs on stdin, one line per ref, and the
# destination is exact. But a managed developer machine may set core.hooksPath
# system-wide (in /etc/gitconfig) to a directory of hook runners belonging to a
# security tool. Those runners do chain to the repository's own
# .git/hooks/<name> and forward the *arguments* — but at least one of them does
# not forward *stdin*, so this hook is invoked with no ref lines at all.
# Exiting 0 on that would make the hook silently inert on exactly the machines
# it is meant to protect, which is worse than not having it: `make
# install-git-hooks` reports success either way.
#
# So when there are no ref lines, this judges by HEAD and its upstream instead.
#
# ⚠️ Substituting that question is wrong in BOTH directions, and the two
# directions arise on DIFFERENT machines — which is why the message names the
# basis it used rather than asserting a cause:
#
#   * Over-refusal, on ANY machine. An empty ref list is not only what a
#     stdin-dropping runner produces: git also supplies one for a push with
#     nothing to send. Measured on the direct path, with no runner involved
#     (`parent=git`), an up-to-date push delivers 0 bytes. So a no-op push while
#     HEAD happens to sit on develop is refused, on an ordinary machine, even
#     though nothing shared was being written. Overridable in one keystroke; the
#     alternative is exiting 0 and being inert where it matters.
#   * Under-refusal, on a redirected machine only. There the destination stops
#     being checked for pushes that do have work to send, so one that DOES write
#     to a shared branch goes through whenever HEAD is not on one. `git push
#     origin HEAD:refs/heads/develop` from a feature branch is allowed, and so are
#     the `HEAD:develop`, `develop` and `--all` forms. All four are refused on the
#     normal path, where the ref list arrives.
#
# The destination is genuinely unknowable in this state: with no ref list, a
# pre-push hook is given only the remote's name and URL, never the refspec. The
# alternative — refusing every push on such a machine — would make the hook
# unusable. So the fallback is the weaker question asked honestly.
#
# What covers the under-refusal, for commands the coding assistant runs, is the
# PreToolUse half: it resolves destinations from the command line and refuses all
# four forms above. What is left genuinely uncovered on such a machine is a direct
# push from a plain shell whose destination names a shared branch while HEAD does
# not.
#
# A genuinely empty push — nothing to send, HEAD not on a shared branch — still
# passes.
#
# Two deliberate behaviours, matching the PreToolUse half:
#   * It does not look at which remote, so pushing develop to a personal fork is
#     refused too.
#   * It keys on the branch name, so `develop` and `main` are shared wherever
#     this hook is installed.
#
# One thing NEITHER half refuses: another session standing in the same working
# directory. Both reason about branches and destinations, not about who else is in
# the directory, so when several assistant sessions share one checkout a `git
# switch` by either moves the tree under the other silently -- and a push from
# there sends whatever that tree now holds. The PreToolUse half REPORTS it (see
# `tenancy_notices` in check_shared_branch.py) and refuses nothing, because a
# per-command hook cannot tell a deliberate switch from a collision. The
# convention that avoids it is a `git worktree` per session (#1087).
#
# Override: ALLOW_SHARED_BRANCH=1 git push ...
#
# This is a local convention, not an enforced control. Branch protection on this
# repository is off and enabling it needs repository admin (issue #933); nothing
# on a contributor's machine can stop a merge made through GitHub's Merge button,
# and `git push --no-verify` skips this hook outright.

set -u

# Only an affirmative value overrides, so ALLOW_SHARED_BRANCH=0 leaves the guard
# on rather than reading as permission.
#
# Saying so matters: exported by a shell profile, an IDE or a CI runner, this
# disables the hook for every push in that environment, and a guard believed on
# and actually off is the failure this file exists to avoid.
case "${ALLOW_SHARED_BRANCH:-}" in
	1 | true | TRUE | True | yes | YES | Yes | y | Y | on | ON | On)
		printf 'shared-branch guard: ALLOW_SHARED_BRANCH is set, so this push is not checked.\n' >&2
		exit 0
		;;
esac

blocked=''
saw_refs=0
basis='the refs git supplied'

# stdin: <local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>, one line per ref.
# A delete (local sha all zeroes) still names its remote ref, so it is covered.
#
# `|| [ -n "$_local_ref" ]` keeps a final line that has no trailing newline: plain
# `read` reports failure at EOF and a bare `while read` would drop that line. git
# terminates every line, so this is belt and braces rather than an observed case.
CR=$(printf '\r')
_local_ref=''
while read -r _local_ref _local_sha remote_ref _remote_sha || [ -n "$_local_ref" ]; do
	saw_refs=1
	# A CR only reaches the ref itself if the ref is the line's last field, which
	# git never does; stripping it costs nothing and makes the answer general.
	remote_ref=${remote_ref%"$CR"}
	case "$remote_ref" in
		refs/heads/develop | refs/heads/main)
			name=${remote_ref#refs/heads/}
			case " $blocked " in
				*" $name "*) ;;
				*) blocked="$blocked $name" ;;
			esac
			;;
	esac
	_local_ref=''
done

if [ "$saw_refs" -eq 0 ]; then
	# No ref lines: either nothing to push, or a hook runner did not forward
	# stdin. Fall back to HEAD and its upstream. Any git failure here leaves
	# both empty, which allows the push.
	#
	# The basis says which two causes are possible rather than picking one.
	# Blaming a runner for what is usually an up-to-date push sends the reader
	# looking for a redirect they may not have.
	basis='HEAD: the ref list was not supplied to this hook, so either this push had nothing to send or a hook runner did not forward it'
	head_branch=$(git symbolic-ref --short HEAD 2>/dev/null) || head_branch=''
	upstream=$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref --symbolic-full-name '@{upstream}' \
		2>/dev/null) || upstream=''
	upstream=${upstream#*/}
	for name in $head_branch $upstream; do
		case "$name" in
			develop | main)
				case " $blocked " in
					*" $name "*) ;;
					*) blocked="$blocked $name" ;;
				esac
				;;
		esac
	done
fi

if [ -z "$blocked" ]; then
	exit 0
fi

printf '\n' >&2
printf 'Refusing to push directly to:%s\n' "$blocked" >&2
printf 'Judged by %s.\n\n' "$basis" >&2
cat >&2 <<'MSG'
Changes reach develop and main through a pull request, so that the lint,
type-check, test, security and dependency gates run and a reviewer sees the
change.

Work on a branch instead:

  git switch -c feature/<name>        # or fix/<name>, docs/<name>
  git push -u origin feature/<name>
  gh pr create --base develop

or re-run with the override in front of the command, to push here deliberately:

  ALLOW_SHARED_BRANCH=1 git push ...
MSG
exit 1
